+# Chapter 33 — 1848: constitution and the First Schleswig War, 1848–1852
+
+*Draft, ten sections. Prose only; apparatus follows the sections.*
+*Notes marked in italics inside the text are drafting flags, not copy.*
+
+---
+
+## A king with no heir
+
+On 9 January 1848 Christian 8. sat up in bed at Amalienborg and wrote out, in a
+hand already going, what he wanted his son to do. He asked for three things: that
+the monarchy be given a common constitution, that Frederik take counsel from
+patriotic men rather than from his friends, and that he contract a suitable
+marriage before the year was out. Eleven days later, on the evening of 20 January,
+the king died of blood poisoning following a bloodletting on his arm. He was
+sixty-one.
+
+The third instruction was the one his son ignored. Frederik 7. was thirty-nine,
+had been married twice, had both marriages dissolved, and had no children; within
+two years he would marry Louise Rasmussen, a milliner and former dancer, and make
+her Countess Danner. The first instruction was carried out within the week, and it
+detonated.
+
+The rescript of 28 January 1848 promised a constitutional order for the whole
+monarchy: a common estates assembly for kingdom and duchies together, to be
+prepared by a gathering of experienced men — eighteen from the kingdom, seven from
+Holstein, six from Slesvig, four from the knighthood, with a further eight named by
+the king from the kingdom and four from each duchy. The common representation
+itself was to have equal numbers from kingdom and duchies, sitting alternately in
+each.
+
+Read that arithmetic slowly, because both national movements did. The kingdom held
+about 1.4 million people and the duchies about 900,000, and the offer gave them the
+same number of seats. To the Copenhagen liberals it meant a German veto over Danish
+affairs. To the Schleswig-Holsteiners it meant Slesvig being counted with Holstein
+in one breath and governed from Copenhagen in the next. A document intended to
+settle the question told both sides they were about to lose.
+
+---
+
+## What Slesvig legally was
+
+The Ribe letter of 1460 and what the nineteenth century made of it belong to
+chapter 19, and the argument about the clause is settled there, not here. What
+matters now is the machinery: three territories, two legal orders, and one man
+about to die without a son.
+
+Holstein was a fief of the Holy Roman Empire, and after 1815 a member state of the
+German Confederation, along with Lauenburg. Its northern boundary was the Eider.
+Slesvig was not in the Confederation. It was a fief of the Danish crown, which
+produced the strangest arrangement in the monarchy: in Slesvig the king of Denmark
+was both the vassal who held the duchy and the lord from whom it was held.
+
+Against that sat the succession. The Kongelov of 1665 is usually described as
+allowing the female line to inherit, and the description is right but too loose.
+The law was agnatic. It gave the crown to the male descendants of Frederik 3., and
+opened the female line only if the male line failed — a fallback nobody expected to
+need for a hundred and eighty years. It also laid down that whatever the king held
+or acquired should remain undivided under the king of Denmark.
+
+The duchies ran on their own rules. Slesvig and Holstein had been cut and recut
+into partial duchies held by collateral branches of the house, each with its own
+succession, and the principle in all of them was agnatic and only agnatic: from man
+to man. Nothing in 1665 changed that, and 1721 did not settle it either. When
+Frederik 4. brought the Gottorp share of Slesvig under the crown and took homage
+for it, he did so without introducing the kingdom's law, the kingdom's
+administration, or Danish as the language of administration. The territory was
+incorporated; the question of whose succession law now ran there was left where it
+had been.
+
+By the 1840s that unanswered question had a date on it. The heirs under the
+Kongelov, in order, were Christian 8.'s brother Prince Ferdinand, who had no
+legitimate children; then his sisters — Juliane Sophie, childless, and Louise
+Charlotte, dead in 1824, married to Landgrave Wilhelm of Hesse-Kassel — and then
+her children. Which is to say: after Frederik 7. the Danish crown passed through a
+woman. Under Holstein's law it could not. The same childless king was therefore
+about to send his kingdom to one man and at least part of his duchies to another,
+and the other was the duke of Augustenborg, whose father had been assembling the
+historical case since 1812 and whose claim became an integrated part of
+Schleswig-Holsteinism from 1844.
+
+Christian 8. tried to close it with the Open Letter of July 1846, which declared
+the Kongelov's succession fully valid for Slesvig and admitted uncertainty for
+parts of Holstein. That is chapter 32's ending. It is worth saying plainly here
+what it did not do: it asserted the Danish reading, it did not prove it.
+
+**Where this account differs from the usual one.** Most popular treatments give
+the dynastic law a paragraph and put the weight on the clash of nationalisms. This
+page gives it a section, because 1864 makes no sense without it. Great powers do
+not send armies over sentiment; they send them over broken instruments. And there
+is a further reason for caution that Danish accounts tend to skip. Working
+source-critically half a century later, the historian Kristian Erslev concluded, in
+1901 and again in 1915, that the Kongelov's provisions had never been valid in
+Slesvig at all — and that the Augustenborg claim was accordingly justified. His
+view has not won general assent and the question remains open. But it means the
+legal case Denmark went to war for in 1848, and lost a duchy over in 1864, was one
+its own best archival scholar later thought was wrong.
+
+---
+
+## March 1848
+
+On 18 March 1848 the German-minded members of the Slesvig and Holstein estates met
+together at Rendsburg and resolved to send the king an ultimatum: a free
+constitution for the duchies, Slesvig admitted to the German Confederation, and a
+Schleswig-Holstein army under its own officers. Granted in full, that would have
+left nothing in common with Denmark but the person of the king. A deputation was
+put on a steamer for Copenhagen.
+
+The news reached Copenhagen on the 20th, and it arrived enlarged. A meeting of the
+national liberals had been called for the 22nd; it was pulled forward two days. That
+afternoon Copenhagen's Borgerrepræsentation debated an address to the king, drafted
+by Orla Lehmann, demanding that the old ministry be replaced by men who would give
+the country a free constitution and bind Slesvig to the kingdom. There was
+resistance at first. After Lehmann spoke there was not.
+
+> **Vignette · Orla Lehmann, the Casino theatre, 20 March 1848**
+>
+> He is thirty-seven, a lawyer, the best speaker in the country and the man who
+> six years earlier had put the phrase *Danmark til Ejderen* into general
+> circulation. The hall is full. Somewhere south of him a deputation from
+> Rendsburg is on the water, and the reports coming in from Kiel that day are
+> alarming and unverified.
+>
+> He tells the hall what he has heard. Their president has just given them the
+> despatches from Kiel; he can add that in all probability the fortress of
+> Rendsburg is already in enemy hands.
+>
+> It is not. There is no rising yet. The provisional government will not be formed
+> until the night of the 23rd, and Rendsburg will not be taken until the morning of
+> the 24th — four days after this speech. Lehmann then answers the objection
+> directly, and it is the most revealing passage in the evening: is this anything
+> but rumour, ask the sensible people, and would it not be wiser to wait until one
+> can see clearly?
+>
+> He does not wait. Voices asking the meeting to hold off until the Rendsburg
+> deputation actually arrives are swept aside. The resolutions pass in what
+> everyone present afterwards described as a revolutionary mood.
+>
+> Orla Lehmann, lawyer and national liberal · the Casino theatre, Copenhagen ·
+> the evening of 20 March 1848 · [-]
+
+On the 21st the Borgerrepræsentation walked to Christiansborg with the address, and
+a crowd of twelve to fifteen thousand walked with it. They were met at the palace
+with the news that the king had already dismissed his ministers that morning. On
+the 22nd the new ministry came together — A.W. Moltke as premier, Bardenfleth at
+justice, Knuth at foreign affairs, Scheel-Plessen for the duchies, Bluhme at trade,
+and, as the mark of the change, Monrad at church and education and Tscherning at
+war. Frederik 7. told them he now regarded himself as a constitutional king.
+Absolutism in Denmark had lasted a hundred and eighty-eight years and ended in a
+week, without a barricade or a shot.
+
+Then the thing Copenhagen had already reacted to actually happened. On the night of
+23–24 March six men met in Kiel and constituted a provisional government for the
+duchies: the lawyers Wilhelm Beseler and Jürgen Bremer for the national liberals,
+Theodor Olshausen, Count Reventlow-Preetz for the nobility, Martin Thorsen Schmidt
+for the Kiel citizenry, and Prince Frederik of Nør, the Augustenborg duke's younger
+brother, as their military man. The proclamation Beseler read from the town hall
+steps at a quarter to six in the morning did not claim independence. It claimed
+that the will of the sovereign was no longer free and the country was without a
+government, and that until the king could decide freely the provisional government
+would carry the responsibility. The formula was Reventlow's, and it was why
+officials and town councils across the duchies accepted a revolutionary body as
+lawful: they were not rebelling, they were rescuing a prisoner.
+
+An hour or so later a special train left Kiel for Rendsburg carrying the Kiel
+garrison and fifty armed citizens under the Prince of Nør. They rang the fire bell.
+The garrison came out of the fortress unarmed to deal with the fire, the officers
+were offered free passage, and almost every soldier joined the rising. Rendsburg
+was the largest garrison in the Danish composite state and it changed hands without
+a casualty, by railway timetable.
+
+Two revolutions in one week, in opposite directions, and the second one was
+reported before it began.
+
+---
+
+## The war begins
+
+The provisional government held most of the duchies within days, but not all of
+them. In Nordslesvig the farming population stayed with the king, and so did the
+town of Haderslev; Sønderborg, Aabenraa and Flensborg split down the middle. That
+division — a duchy in which loyalty ran north to south rather than along any
+administrative line — is the fact the next sixteen years were fought over, and it
+was visible in the first fortnight.
+
+Danish troops crossed into Slesvig on 29 March. On 9 April about eleven thousand
+men under Hedemann met a Schleswig-Holstein force of some six thousand under
+General Krohn at Bov, north of the modern border, and broke it; roughly a sixth of
+the enemy were taken prisoner and the rest went back to Rendsburg. Within two days
+the Danish army stood on the line of the Slien and the Danevirke. Denmark had
+apparently won its civil war in a month.
+
+It had not, because the war stopped being a civil war. Prussia and the German
+Confederation came in on the insurgents' side, and on Easter Day, 23 April, General
+Wrangel attacked the Danish position outside the town of Slesvig. How large a force
+he brought is genuinely disputed: some accounts give thirty-two thousand men and
+seventy-four guns, while a reckoning from the units actually engaged puts the Danes
+against nearer seventeen thousand. About twelve thousand Danes held for a day and
+then went back to Als. Their losses in killed, wounded and captured were 816; the
+other side's, 461.
+
+The following day the rear-guard was ambushed at Oversø, south of Flensborg, by
+Mecklenburg dragoons and Hanoverian infantry, and fifty men were taken. Then the
+Danes won again — at Nybøl on 28 May and at Dybbøl on 5 June — which delighted
+Denmark and alarmed everybody else. Russia had no intention of letting Prussia
+cross the Kongeå into the kingdom proper. Under British and Swedish mediation the
+fighting was suspended by the armistice of Malmö on 26 August 1848.
+
+That armistice is where the war's real shape shows. It was ratified on 16 September
+by the Frankfurt National Assembly — a parliament whose first act of foreign policy
+had been to claim Schleswig-Holstein for Germany, and which now voted to accept
+terms it had no power to enforce. The provisional government was wound up on 22
+October and replaced by a joint Danish-Prussian administration sitting at Gottorp.
+Nobody had conceded anything. Everybody had discovered that the question would be
+decided somewhere other than the battlefield.
+
+---
+
+## The soldier's war
+
+Until 1848 the Danish army was recruited almost entirely from one class. Conscription
+fell on the peasantry: on farmers, cottagers and their sons, entered in the
+*lægdsrulle* parish by parish. Townsmen, burghers, officials and the nobility were
+exempt, either by birth or by having bought the exemption with a payment into the
+Landmilicefonden or the Søindrulleringsfonden, and they supplied the officers. The
+apparatus that did the counting was the one chapter 29 described: the muster roll
+built in the 1780s to keep track of men who were tied to the soil.
+
+> **Vignette · Morten Jørgensen, dragoon, Stade, September 1848**
+>
+> He was born on 8 June 1825 at Aldal in Rold parish, in Rold Skov in Himmerland,
+> the son of a *husmand*. The family lived off small plots, kitchen gardens and
+> clogs: they cut beech billets and carved them, five pairs on an ordinary day and
+> seven on a good one. The census of 1845 lists him at home with his parents as a
+> clog-maker, one of seventeen in the three parishes around the forest. His mother
+> had died when he was six, of an infection after a tooth was pulled badly.
+>
+> He is called up in December 1847, before there is a war to fight, because
+> conscription takes farmers and cottagers and he is of that estate. At the
+> barracks at Randers he is given a horse, a sabre, a carbine, a red uniform, a
+> helmet, and — by the cavalry's habit of naming men after the places they come
+> from — the name Aldahl. His pay is fourteen skilling a day.
+>
+> On 23 or 24 April 1848, in the confusion of the retreat from Slesvig, he is
+> wounded and taken at Oversø. The Hanoverians march their prisoners to Stade,
+> west of Hamburg, where seven hundred Danes are quartered in the barracks and
+> decently treated. Morten Jørgensen dies there in early September, in his
+> twenty-third year.
+>
+> His death is entered in the muster roll, and underneath it someone has written,
+> neatly, *Viborg 53 X 222*. That is where the replacement came from. Four or five
+> weeks later the vacant horse and saddle were filled from the fifty-third lægd of
+> Viborg amt, arrival roll X, number 222 — Jens Peter Sørensen, born 1825 at
+> Højslev near Skive, who survived the war. Nothing else of Morten Jørgensen
+> survives: not a letter, not a photograph, not a uniform button.
+>
+> Morten Jørgensen Aldahl, dragoon, 5th Dragoon Regiment, 3rd squadron, no. 19 ·
+> Stade, kingdom of Hanover · September 1848 · [n]
+
+The war changed who had to go. A temporary ordinance of 23 September 1848 pulled in
+men who had been exempt, for the duration; then, after three days of debate in the
+constituent assembly in December, the Law on Universal Conscription of 12 February
+1849 made it permanent. The cut it made was not between town and country but
+between one birth year and the next. Every man born in 1826 or later lost whatever
+exemption he had, whether it came from his birth or had been bought with a payment
+into the funds; every man born before 1 January 1826 kept his. Every male was to be
+entered in the *lægdsrulle* as soon as he was confirmed, the parish priest reporting
+the confirmed boys. The obligation was written into the constitution four months
+later.
+
+Two things follow, and both are worth saying because the phrase "universal
+conscription" carries neither of them. The first is that a man could still buy his
+way out by paying another to serve in his place, and that survived until 1867 —
+through this war and the next one. The second is that the institution which now
+enrolled every boy in Denmark was the same register, built for the *stavnsbånd*,
+that had once existed to stop men leaving.
+
+Nobody in the state counted the dead. The list was made privately, by Asser Daniel
+Cohen, catechist to the Mosaic congregations of Funen and Lolland-Falster, a
+bookseller in Odense who had already published, at his own expense, the first
+history of the Jews in Denmark. His register of the fallen gives 533 for 1848, 1,381
+for 1849 and 1,872 for 1850: **3,786** men, more than the 2,978 Denmark would lose in
+1864. His son continued the work after that war. It is still the principal name
+register for both.
+
+---
+
+## 5 June 1849
+
+The constituent assembly was elected in October 1848, with a minority of members
+named by the king, and sat at Christiansborg through the winter arguing about who a
+citizen was. On 5 June 1849 Frederik 7. signed what it produced.
+
+The Junigrundlov gave Denmark a two-chamber Rigsdag — a Folketing and a Landsting,
+elected in 1849 on the same qualifications — a government answerable to it, and a
+list of rights: freedom of speech, of assembly and of association, an end to
+arbitrary imprisonment, an end to censorship. Measured against 28 January 1848 it
+was an enormous distance travelled in seventeen months.
+
+Then the qualifications. To vote a man had to be unblemished in reputation, hold
+Danish *indfødsret*, be thirty years old, have his own household, not be in another's
+service without one, and never have received poor relief that had not been repaid.
+The result was that **15 per cent of the whole population** could vote, which is
+**72.8 per cent of all men over thirty**. Popular speech named the excluded as the
+seven F's: *fruentimmere, folkehold, fattige, fremmede, fallenter, fjolser,
+forbrydere* — women, servants, paupers, foreigners, bankrupts, the certified insane
+and the convicted. Six of those seven categories would take between sixty-six and a
+hundred and sixty years to clear.
+
+What the first election looked like is worth having as a number rather than as an
+impression. On 4 December 1849 a hundred seats were filled. In forty-three of them
+there was no ballot at all, the single candidate being carried by acclamation; in
+the fifty-seven that were contested, 37,903 men voted out of 116,553 entitled. A
+third of the electorate of a third of the men of a country turned out, in less than
+two-thirds of the constituencies, at the first free election in Danish history.
+
+One more clause matters more than any of this, and almost nobody reads it. The
+constitution carried an opening reservation that everything concerning the position
+of the duchy of Slesvig would wait until peace was concluded. It never came into
+force south of the Kongeå.
+
+> **Vignette · Mathilde Fibiger, Lolland, December 1850**
+>
+> She is nineteen when she takes the post and twenty when the book comes out. In
+> October 1849 she goes to Lolland as governess to a forest ranger's family at
+> Maltrupgård near Sakskøbing, which is one of the very few kinds of paid work an
+> unmarried woman of her class can do without disgrace. She is a colonel's
+> daughter. She has no vote, no capacity to hold property in her own right after
+> marriage, and no prospect of either.
+>
+> In the ranger's house she writes twelve letters from an invented woman called
+> Clara Raphael to an invented friend, and sends the manuscript to Johan Ludvig
+> Heiberg — the most powerful literary figure in Denmark, and the husband of the
+> actress who is chapter 32's vignette. He places it with Reitzel, writes a long
+> and carefully soothing preface, and it appears in December 1850 with 1851 on the
+> title page, in eight hundred copies. Within a year it has drawn some twenty-five
+> newspaper pieces and ten pamphlets. It is the first time a woman in Denmark has
+> publicly demanded equality, and her phrase for it, *Damernes Emancipation*,
+> enters the language.
+>
