+# Chapter 34 — 1864, 1863–1864
+
+*Draft, ten sections. Prose only; apparatus follows the sections.*
+*Notes marked in italics inside the text are drafting flags, not copy.*
+
+---
+
+## A king three days on the throne
+
+On 13 November 1863 the Rigsråd passed a new constitution for the kingdom and
+Slesvig together, and left Holstein out. Two days later Frederik 7. died without
+signing it, at Glücksburg Castle in the duchy — the last king of the Oldenburg
+line dying in the house that gave its name to the dynasty about to replace him.
+
+His successor was proclaimed from the balcony of Christiansborg the next day by
+the prime minister, C.C. Hall. Christian 9. was forty-five, born at Gottorp,
+raised in the borderland, a conservative believer in the composite state who spoke
+Danish with a German accent, and he owed his crown entirely to the Treaty of
+London — the instrument the document on his desk broke.
+
+He was not the only claimant to move. On 16 November, the day after Frederik 7.
+died, Prince Frederik of Augustenborg formed a government in exile at Gotha and
+proclaimed himself Friedrich VIII, duke of Slesvig and Holstein. His family had
+renounced in 1852 and had been paid to do it. In the duchies he was received as
+the rightful heir, and within weeks portraits of him were being sold in Kiel.
+
+It is worth being clear about which body had passed the thing he was being asked
+to sign. It was not the Rigsdag of 1849 but the Rigsråd, the joint assembly of
+kingdom and duchies created by the composite-state constitution of 1855 — the
+institution designed to hold the monarchy together, voting to take it apart.
+
+> **Vignette · Christian 9., Copenhagen, 18 November 1863**
+>
+> He has been king for three days. In front of him is a constitution his
+> predecessor did not sign, which joins Slesvig to Denmark and leaves Holstein
+> outside — and he knows, better than almost anyone in the country, that Denmark
+> undertook in 1851 and 1852 never to do this. He knows it because the same
+> settlement is why he is king at all.
+>
+> He takes two days to decide. The delay is read in Copenhagen as sympathy for the
+> composite state, which it is, and the national liberal press is waiting. There
+> are demonstrations. There are calls for him to go. His Danishness is
+> questioned in print by people who had not questioned it a week earlier.
+>
+> On the 18th he signs, and adds a remark that he fears the national consequences.
+> He will consider abdicating within the year. He will reign for forty-three more
+> and outlive every politician who pressed him.
+>
+> Christian 9., king of Denmark for three days · Copenhagen · 18 November 1863 ·
+> [-]
+
+---
+
+## Why the powers did not come
+
+The Danish expectation in the winter of 1863 was that Denmark would not be left
+alone. It was left alone, and the reasons are worth setting out plainly, because
+the Danish account of 1864 has generally preferred betrayal to the alternative.
+
+The alternative is that Denmark had put itself in the wrong. The composite-state
+constitution of 1855 had been rejected in Holstein and declared invalid there and
+in Lauenburg by the German Confederation in 1857, which left the monarchy
+governing two of its provinces under a document those provinces did not recognise.
+The November Constitution was Copenhagen's answer, and it was the specific thing
+Denmark had promised in the notes of 1851–52 not to do: it bound Slesvig to the
+kingdom more closely than Holstein. Every foreign ministry in Europe could read
+that, and did.
+
+So the German Confederation moved first and legally, executing federal law in
+Holstein and Lauenburg in December 1863. Then Prussia and Austria stepped outside
+the Confederation and moved on their own account, crossing the Eider into Slesvig
+— which was not federal territory — on 1 February 1864. What began as an
+enforcement action became a war of conquest between those two dates, and the
+transition was Bismarck's work. He had no interest in Friedrich VIII or in an
+independent Schleswig-Holstein. He wanted the duchies for Prussia, and a Danish
+breach of treaty gave him a way to take them with Austria's signature on it.
+
+Britain had encouraged Denmark and would not act without France. France wanted
+compensation on the Rhine and was not offered any. Russia, whose Tsar had been a
+guarantor in 1852 and whose interest in 1848 had been decisive, had no interest
+now in propping up a state that had broken the settlement Russia had imposed.
+Sweden-Norway talked about Scandinavian brotherhood and sent nobody.
+
+The lesson Denmark drew from 1848 to 1850 was that the balance of Europe would
+always protect it. That was true while Denmark kept the terms the balance was
+built on. It stopped being true in November 1863, and nobody in Copenhagen
+appears to have thought the two facts were connected.
+
+---
+
+## Dannevirke, 5–6 February
+
+The army that marched south in January 1864 marched to a rampart. The Dannevirke
+had held the neck of Jutland on and off for a thousand years, and in the Danish
+imagination it was a wall. In fact it was a badly maintained line of earthworks
+thirty kilometres long, designed for an army twice the size of the one holding it,
+with a frozen Slien on one flank that an enemy could walk across.
+
+On 2 February the fighting at Mysunde showed what that meant: the position could
+be turned. On the 4th the king and the war minister left. That evening the
+commanding general, Christian de Meza, held a council of war.
+
+The council had a document in front of it. The War Ministry's own instruction of
+22 January had told de Meza that what mattered was still to possess, in the coming
+spring, "a capable and battle-ready army". The council concluded that the army
+could only remain capable and battle-ready by withdrawing to the flank positions
+at Dybbøl and Fredericia. It voted to go.
+
+The retreat began on the 5th. The fortress artillery was left in the works so the
+enemy would see guns where there were no longer men; instructions went out unit by
+unit rather than as a single plan, to keep it quiet; the last outposts left the
+forward ground between eleven and midnight. It was snowing, the roads were sheet
+ice, and the men had been sleeping outdoors in it for weeks. It took the Prussians
+and Austrians about eight hours to notice that the wall in front of them was empty.
+
+That was precisely what the enemy least wanted. Wrangel had instructions from
+Bismarck and the war minister von Roon to destroy the Danish army before it could
+reach its flank positions, and the withdrawal denied him it. Austrian cavalry
+caught the rearguard south of Flensborg, and at Sankelmark on the 6th there was a
+short, savage fight with bayonets against an Austrian brigade: **53 Danish dead and
+95 Austrian.** The army got away.
+
+Shortly after one in the morning on the 6th, in Flensborg, de Meza was handed a
+telegram from the war minister in Copenhagen. *I do not acknowledge that the
+Commanding General of the Army has complied with the Instruction, and I reserve
+investigation accordingly.*
+
+He had complied with it. That is what the instruction said and what the council
+minuted. It made no difference. The public had been told nothing and expected a
+battle at the Dannevirke; what it got was a retreat in the dark, and Copenhagen
+smelled treason. Crowds demanded the high command's dismissal, and a good many
+people believed the king had ordered the retreat himself, which he had not.
+Monrad dismissed de Meza within days. He took full responsibility, never recovered
+his position, and died the following year, bitter and alone — a man who had begun
+as a language teacher at the officers' school and was known chiefly for his
+extraordinary clothes.
+
+The withdrawal from the Dannevirke was the best decision anyone made in the war.
+
+---
+
+## Dybbøl
+
+The war reached Jutland at the end of February, and it reached it as small groups
+of horsemen on winter roads.
+
+> **Vignette · Niels Kjeldsen, south of Højen, 28 February 1864**
+>
+> He is twenty-three, a farmer's son from Bæksgårde in Give parish, and he has
+> been a soldier for eight months. He did his conscript service from June 1863
+> with the 6th Dragoon Regiment's 4th squadron **at Itzehoe in Holstein** — a
+> Danish peasant garrisoned in the German duchy he is now defending against
+> Germans.
+>
+> On a Sunday morning six men ride out from Højen kro, south of Vejle, along the
+> Kolding road. Nine Prussian hussars surprise them. Two of his companions are
+> caught and surrender. Kjeldsen is a good swordsman; he is overtaken, refuses
+> quarter, and defends himself with his sabre while still riding. A pursuing
+> officer shoots him through the head from behind. The skirmish attracts no
+> particular notice at the time.
+>
+> Seventeen years later it does. In 1881 the first issue of *Vort Forsvar*, in the
+> middle of the constitutional struggle, printed a different version: he had
+> stood and faced them, beaten three Prussians with cold steel, and been shot in
+> the back by a coward whose own regiment then disowned him. In 1901 Frants
+> Henningsen painted that scene for the museum at Frederiksborg. In 1902 Karl
+> Larsen took it apart, using five contemporary accounts and ten surviving
+> eyewitnesses, and the painting came down in 1912 over the army's protests. It
+> hangs in the dragoon barracks at Holstebro now.
+>
+> What survives every version: he was shot from behind, which is what happens to
+> a man riding away; and he had refused to surrender, which is what he was
+> supposed to do. The Prussian who killed him was congratulated by his superiors,
+> presented to two visiting princes, and decorated.
+>
+> Niels Kjeldsen, dragoon, 6th Dragoon Regiment · Blåkær skov, south of Højen ·
+> 28 February 1864 · [n]
+
+The army meanwhile sat down at Dybbøl. The position was a half-moon of ten small
+earth redoubts running three kilometres from Vemmingbund to Als Sund, built by
+Danish engineers in 1861 and 1862, with concrete powder magazines and wooden
+blockhouses. It had been designed as an offensive flank position, from which an
+army could threaten the supply lines of an enemy pushing north into Jutland. It
+had not been designed to be besieged, and it was not finished. The first troops
+arrived on 7 February and spent the month digging.
+
+What happened next was an engineering operation, not a battle. On 15 March the
+Prussians emplaced rifled breech-loading guns on Broager Land, across the water to
+the south, and from the twin towers of Broager church their staff watched the fall
+of shot and corrected it. Danish artillery could not reach them. From 2 April
+Sønderborg was shelled and much of the town was reduced to rubble. On 11 April
+four thousand shells fell on the redoubts in a day. The Prussians dug parallel
+trenches, each closer than the last, until the forward one was three hundred metres
+from the Danish line.
+
+For the men in the redoubts this was five weeks of being shelled without being
+able to reply. They dug at night, repaired what had been knocked down in the day,
+and were shelled again. The one thing that could answer Broager was the ironclad
+*Rolf Krake*, bought in Glasgow the year before — four heavy guns in two revolving
+armoured turrets, sail as a reserve if the steam failed, and the beginning of a
+different kind of navy. She came into Vemmingbund and fired on the Prussian flank
+and was driven off, and she could not be everywhere.
+
+On 18 April the bombardment began at four in the morning and ran for six hours.
+More than eight thousand shells fell. At ten the storm troops went in, and the
+central and southern redoubts were gone in half an hour. The Danish 8th Brigade
+counter-attacked under Colonel Scharffenberg, fought its way forward to Dybbøl
+Mill and Langdamgård, and broke after an hour and a half. What was left of the
+army crossed to Als.
+
+How many died there is not known, and the disagreement is not a small one. The
+official Danish return for 18 April gives 379 killed, 646 missing, 1,250 wounded
+and about 2,500 taken prisoner. German accounts give 3,600 Danes killed and 1,200
+Prussians. A reckoning published at the hundred and fiftieth anniversary gives
+1,669 Danish dead and wounded against 1,201 Prussian, with 3,131 unwounded
+prisoners and deserters. The National Museum gives 700 dead. Historians attribute
+the spread to the ordinary practice of both sides: understate your own losses,
+overstate the enemy's. This page reports the figures and does not average them.
+
+---
+
+## The London Conference
+
+A conference opened in London on 20 April, two days after Dybbøl fell, and a
+ceasefire followed on 12 May. Britain, France, Russia, Prussia, Austria, Sweden
+and the German Confederation sat down with Denmark to divide Slesvig.
+
+Three days before the ceasefire, Denmark won the war's only fleet action, and it
+is worth putting beside the conference table because of how little it bought.
+
+Denmark had held the sea from the start. The navy was the one arm of the Danish
+service that was not outclassed, and it had blockaded the North Sea and Baltic
+ports of the German states since February, which hurt. On 9 May a squadron of
+three ships under Edouard Suenson — the screw frigates *Niels Juel* and *Jylland*
+and the corvette *Hejmdal* — met an Austrian-Prussian force under Wilhelm von
+Tegetthoff about ten sea miles south-east of Heligoland, then a British island and
+neutral. The Prussian gunboats were too slow to engage. The two Austrian frigates
+did, the Danish gunnery set the flagship *Schwarzenberg*'s foretop alight three
+times, and the third fire could not be put out. Tegetthoff ran for the neutral
+three-mile limit, where Suenson could not follow.
+
+*Jylland* fired six hundred and eleven rounds that afternoon and took eighteen
+direct hits. Fourteen Danish sailors were killed and fifty-four wounded, against
+thirty-six killed and a hundred and eight wounded on the other side. The squadron
+went to Kristiansand and buried its dead, and Suenson came home to Copenhagen a
+national hero and to a criticism that has never quite gone away: he did not wait
+outside the limit, and the Austrians slipped out in the night to Cuxhaven.
+
+The blockade held. After Heligoland the enemy stopped trying to break it. It made
+no difference to anything. The war was being decided by rifled artillery on a hill
+in Sundeved, and a country that controls the sea and cannot hold a hill loses.
+
+At the table, Denmark negotiated as though none of April had happened. The
+position held by Monrad's government began at the Eider — the whole of Slesvig to
+the kingdom — which was the war aim rather than an opening bid. Christian 9. tried
+as long as he could to keep the composite state whole and had to give that up too.
+When partition became unavoidable the argument moved to where the line should run,
+and there it stuck: Denmark would not go south of Flensborg, Prussia would not come
+north of it. Britain proposed arbitration. Denmark refused, on the reasoning that
+an arbitrator might award less than the Danish line.
+
+The conference broke up at the end of June with nothing agreed. This is the point
+in 1864 at which a Danish decision, rather than a Prussian one, determined the
+outcome — and the decision was to go on asking for what the country had already
+shown it could not hold. A line drawn in June would have run well south of the one
+Denmark got in October, and not far from the one it would get in 1920. That is not
+hindsight. It was said at the table, in English, by people trying to help.
+
+---
+
+## Als, and Jutland occupied
+
+The fighting resumed on 26 June. Three days later, before dawn on the 29th,
+Prussian troops crossed Als Sund in rowing boats from the fishing hamlet of
+Sottrupskov — about a hundred and sixty-five boats in the first wave — against a
+Danish force on Als of some twelve thousand men.
+
+Danish resistance was broken completely. About three thousand men were killed,
+wounded or captured; one account puts it at 216 dead and 2,932 wounded, taken or
+missing. Prussian losses were around 372. Everything that could be got away was
+evacuated to Funen. Als was the last part of Slesvig in Danish hands and it was
+lost in four hours.
+
+Austrian and Prussian forces had meanwhile taken Jutland. They had crossed the
+Kongeå in March and they went the whole way: by July the occupation reached
+Skagen, and for the first time since the Swedish wars of chapter 24 a foreign army
+was quartered in every part of the peninsula. What that meant locally was
+requisitions — hay, oats, horses, wagons, quarters for men — paid for in receipts
+against a government that had lost, and a civil administration that went on
+collecting Danish taxes for a Danish state whose writ had stopped at the county
+boundary.
+
+On 3 July, at Lundby near Aalborg, a Danish force attacked entrenched Prussian
+infantry across open ground with the bayonet. The Prussians were armed with the
+needle gun and were lying down. The Danes lost about a third of their strength in
+a few minutes without reaching the position, and it is the one place in the war
+where the rifle explanation is simply correct. It was the last action of the war
+and it decided nothing whatever.
+
+Monrad's government fell. A new ministry signed an armistice on 20 July. By then
+the Danish army had no capacity left, the country north of the Kongeå was under
+occupation, and the question at the peace table was not what Denmark would keep
+but what the victors would take.
+
+---
+
+## Vienna, 30 October
+
+A preliminary peace was signed on 1 August under the threat of resumed fighting.
+The treaty itself was settled in Vienna and signed on 30 October 1864.
+
+Its central article is short, and it is worth reading in the shape it was written:
+the king of Denmark renounces all his rights to the duchies of Slesvig, Holstein
+and Lauenburg in favour of the Emperor of Austria and the King of Prussia, and
+undertakes to recognise whatever dispositions those two majesties may make
+concerning them. The cession of Slesvig covers every island belonging to the duchy
+as well as the mainland.
+
+Read it again. Denmark did not cede the duchies to Schleswig-Holstein, or to
+Germany, or to the duke whose claim had been the pretext. It ceded them to two
+monarchs personally, to do with as they liked. Friedrich VIII, who had been
+received in Kiel as the rightful heir, received nothing. Sovereignty passed to a
+Prussian–Austrian condominium; the two powers quarrelled over the administration
+of the spoils; and in 1866 they fought each other over it, after which the duchies
+became provinces of Prussia in 1867.
+
+Two things went home before the treaty did. Some three thousand Danish prisoners
+were still in German hands in the summer of 1864, held at Magdeburg and elsewhere,
+and they came back through the autumn to a country that had no particular use for
+them. And the state that had defaulted in 1813 and spent forty years rebuilding
+its credit had now fought an eight-month war it could not pay for out of revenue,
+which is a debt chapter 35's economy is built on top of.
+
+There were border adjustments, and they ran both ways. The royal enclaves inside
+Slesvig — Danish crown territory scattered through the duchy since the Middle Ages
+— went to Austria and Prussia. In exchange, Ribe Herred, Nørre Tyrstrup Herred
+south of Kolding, and the island of Ærø came into the kingdom. The old Kongeå
+line, which had divided kingdom from duchy since the fourteenth century, became a
+state frontier for the first time.
+
+---
+
+## What was ceded, and who
+
+How much Denmark lost in 1864 depends on what is being counted, and the published
+answers do not agree. The area is given as a third of the monarchy by one
+authority and two-fifths by the same authority elsewhere. The population loss is
+given as forty per cent. The number of Danish-speakers who passed under German
+rule is given as **two hundred thousand** in some accounts and **a hundred and
+seventy thousand** in others.
+
+Two things can be stated exactly, because Danmarks Statistik computed them at the
+time. The parishes of Ribe Amt ceded under the treaty held **13,053** people at the
+census of 1860. The territory that came the other way — Ærø and the smaller
+mainland stretches — held **20,864**. The land Denmark gained in the settlement
+therefore held **7,811 more people than the land it gave up in the same
+transaction**, which is a fact nobody remembers and which changes nothing about
+the scale of the loss: the kingdom stood at about 1.6 million in 1860 and the
+monarchy at roughly two and a half.
+
+The treaty gave the people in the ceded territories a choice. They could keep
+Danish *indfødsret* and stay where they were, provided they did not become a
+nuisance to the authorities — the *optanter*, who would spend the next fifty years
+discovering what that condition meant in practice, and whose sons would be
+conscripted into an army they had opted out of belonging to.
+
+Two years later Prussia and Austria went to war and Prussia won, and the Peace of
+Prague of August 1866 carried an Article 5: the northern districts of Slesvig were
+to be ceded back to Denmark if their population voted for it. It was a Prussian
+concession to Napoleon III rather than to Denmark, no vote was ever held, and in
+1878 Prussia and Austria abrogated the article between themselves. The promise
+lasted twelve years and was extinguished by the two parties who had made it, in
+the absence of the party it concerned.
+
+Counting is also what the year looked like from inside a house in Copenhagen.
+
+> **Vignette · Ilia Fibiger, Lægeforeningens Boliger, 1864**
+>
+> She is forty-six, and she is the elder sister of chapter 33's vignette by
+> thirteen years. In 1853, when cholera was killing Copenhagen, she walked into
+> Almindelig Hospital as a volunteer — a woman from the senior military official
+> class presenting herself for unpaid duty on the ward with the highest mortality
+> in the city — and from 1854 to 1860 she was employed as Denmark's first
+> professional nurse. She published plays and tales and a novel, always
+> anonymously.
+>
+> The one man she loved was a naval officer named Osvald Marstrand, and he was
+> killed at Eckernförde in 1849, in the war of the previous chapter. She never
+> married and never got over it, and it is in everything she wrote.
+>
+> By 1864 she is living in Lægeforeningens Boliger on the edge of Copenhagen —
+> the terraces the doctors built after the cholera, the country's first social
+> housing, still standing and now called Brumleby. She is bringing up other
+> people's daughters there. In the year the state loses two fifths of its
+> territory she takes in three more: Ane Sophie, Louise and Johanne Mathilde, all
+> arriving in 1864. Another follows in 1865. Six of them end up carrying her
+> surname, though the family does not accept them after her death.
+>
+> It uses her up. She dies on 10 June 1867, aged forty-nine, worn out in the
+> housing scheme. Her poems are published posthumously that year, and the
+> nine-page life at the front of them is written by Mathilde — the sister
+> respectable Copenhagen had cut in 1851, and who had had Ilia's disapproval for
+> causing it.
+>
+> Ilia Marie Fibiger, nurse and writer · Lægeforeningens Boliger, Copenhagen ·
+> 1864 · [f]
+
+---
+
+## The constitution of 1866
+
+Denmark came out of the war with two constitutions and no territory to apply the
+second one to. The June constitution of 1849 governed the kingdom; the November
+constitution of 1863 had been written for the kingdom and Slesvig, and Slesvig was
+gone. Something had to be repealed, and the price of repealing it was set by the
+people who had wanted the June constitution amended since the day it was signed.
+
+The mechanics matter, because they are where the leverage was. The November
+Constitution could not simply be repealed: it was the constitution under which the
+Rigsråd sat, and the Rigsråd had to consent to its own abolition. That gave the
+Landsting of the Rigsråd — the chamber the 1863 document had already given a
+restricted franchise — a veto over the terms on which Denmark returned to a single
+constitution. The conservative and landowning members used it, and what they asked
+for in exchange for winding up the joint institutions was the June constitution
+amended in their favour. The argument ran through 1865 in both chambers of the
+Rigsråd and was settled by a joint committee.
+
+The revision was passed in 1866 and came into force on 28 July. The Folketing kept
+universal suffrage and was left alone. The Landsting was rebuilt. It would have
+sixty-six members: twelve chosen by the king for life, one by the Faroese Lagting,
+one by Bornholm's amtsråd, and the remainder elected indirectly through electors —
+with **half the electors chosen by the ordinary voters and the other half by the
+highest taxpayers alone.**
+
+The compromise that produced that split was proposed by D.G. Monrad, who had
+drafted the June constitution seventeen years earlier and who had just resigned as
+prime minister after losing the war. Its architect and beneficiary was the
+landowner J.B.S. Estrup, interior minister in the Frijs ministry, who took one of
+the twelve royally appointed seats and held it, with one interruption, until his
+death in 1913.
+
+Two details did the real work. The distribution of seats between town and country
+was frozen into the law itself, with no provision for future shifts in population,
+which guaranteed the landowners a floor of about eighteen seats in perpetuity as
+the towns grew around them. And nothing whatever was written down about what
+should happen if the two chambers disagreed. Estrup was asked about this and
+answered that fear of the constitutional vacuum would surely bring them to
+compromise.
+
+It did not. In 1872 the united Venstre took a majority in the Folketing while
+Højre held the Landsting, and the machinery locked. What follows is thirty years of
+constitutional struggle, government by provisional law, and a king insisting on
+choosing ministers a parliamentary majority did not want — chapter 36's subject,
+built here, in 1866, out of the wreckage of a war.
+
+That is the part of 1864 that is usually a footnote. Denmark lost two-fifths of
+its territory to Prussia and Austria, and lost the franchise of its upper house to
+its own landowners, and only the first of those was done by foreigners.
+
+---
+
+## What was to be won inward
+
+In 1866 a group of men founded Det danske Hedeselskab to plant and drain the
+Jutland heath, and its first director was an engineer officer named Enrico Mylius
+Dalgas, who had served in both wars. What they did was real: over the following
+half-century the heath was substantially converted, and the argument that a country
+could compensate for lost ground by improving the ground it kept is the argument
+Denmark told itself for a generation.
+
+The sentence it told itself with was not Dalgas's. *Hvad udad tabes, skal indad
+vindes* — what is lost without shall be won within — appears nowhere in anything
+he is known to have said or written. It was composed by the poet H.P. Holst for a
+medal struck for the Nordic Industry and Art Exhibition in Copenhagen in the summer
+of 1872, and what he actually wrote was *For hvert et Tab igjen Erstatning findes;
+hvad udad tabtes, det maa indad vindes* — for every loss a compensation is found;
+what was lost without must be won within. Past tense, and about Danish industry,
+not about heather. The thought was not original either: Tegnér had proposed
+reconquering Finland inside Sweden's borders in 1811, two years after Sweden lost
+it.
+
+H.P. Holst had written something else, in 1850. *Slumrer sødt i Slesvigs Jord* —
+sleep sweetly in Slesvig's earth — was the song Denmark sang over the dead of the
+Three Years' War, the men in the ground at Flensborg.
+
+Chapter 33 closed at that cemetery. In 1864 the Prussians came to it, took down the
+bronze lion that had stood over the graves since 1862, and carried it to Berlin,
+where they set it up outside their cadet school. The Danes buried underneath it
+stayed where they were, on the other side of a border, and the monument that said
+*the Danish people raised this memorial* spent the next eighty years in Germany.
+
+---
+
+# Chapter 34 — apparatus
+
+*Draft. Glossary blocks are per-section (`Danish terms in this section`), so they
+are listed under the section they attach to. Question tiers follow the series:
+Recall, Causal, Counterfactual, Contested.*
+
+---
+
+## Danish terms, by section
+
+**§01 — a king three days on the throne**
+
+- **Novemberforfatningen** — the November Constitution of 18 November 1863, a
+  common constitution for the kingdom and Slesvig with Holstein left outside. The
+  direct cause of the war.
+
+**§02 — why the powers did not come**
+
+- **forbundseksekution** — federal execution: the German Confederation enforcing
+  its own law against a member state, carried out in Holstein and Lauenburg in
+  December 1863. Legally quite different from the invasion of Slesvig that
+  followed, which is why Prussia and Austria had to step outside the Confederation
+  to do it.
+- *`helstat` is glossed in chapter 33 — cross-reference, do not re-gloss.*
+
+**§03 — Dannevirke**
+
+- **Dannevirke** — the earthwork across the neck of Jutland, begun in the eighth
+  century (chapter 5) and the frontier of chapter 12. In 1864 a thirty-kilometre
+  line requiring an army twice the size of the one that held it.
+
+**§04 — Dybbøl**
+
+- **skanse** — a redoubt: an enclosed earthwork for guns and men. Ten of them made
+  the Dybbøl position, built 1861–62 in a three-kilometre arc.
+- **bagladegevær** — breech-loading rifle, loaded lying down. The Prussian needle
+  gun against the Danish muzzle-loader is the standard explanation for 1864 and it
+  explains less than it is asked to: see the myth-check.
+
+**§05 — the London Conference**
+
+- **Ejderpolitik** — *glossed in chapter 32; the position Denmark was still
+  holding in London after losing the battle that made it impossible.*
+
+**§06 — Als**
+
+- **Lundby** — the last engagement of the war, 3 July 1864: a bayonet attack
+  across open ground against entrenched breech-loaders.
+
+**§07 — Vienna**
+
+- **Wienerfreden** — the Peace of Vienna, 30 October 1864.
+- **kongerigske enklaver** — the royal enclaves: parcels of Danish crown territory
+  lying inside the duchy of Slesvig since the Middle Ages, traded away in the
+  settlement.
+
+**§08 — what was ceded, and who**
+
+- **optant** — a person in the ceded territories who chose to keep Danish
+  *indfødsret* and remain living where they were. The choice was permitted by the
+  treaty and made conditional on not becoming a nuisance to the authorities.
+- **Pragfredens artikel 5** — Article 5 of the Peace of Prague, August 1866,
+  promising that northern Slesvig would return to Denmark if its people voted for
+  it. No vote was held; Prussia and Austria abrogated the article in 1878.
+
+**§09 — the constitution of 1866**
+
+- **Den gennemsete Grundlov** — "the revised constitution", in force 28 July 1866.
+  Also called the July constitution.
+- **privilegeret valgret** — privileged franchise: the arrangement by which half
+  the electors who chose the Landsting were themselves chosen by the highest
+  taxpayers alone.
+
+**§10 — what was to be won inward**
+
+- **Hedeselskabet** — the Danish Heath Society, founded 1866 to drain and plant the
+  Jutland heath.
+- **skuemønt** — an exhibition medal, struck to be sold and looked at rather than
+  spent. The one struck in 1872 carries the sentence this section is about.
+
+---
+
+## Meanwhile in Europe
+
+**Geneva, 22 August 1864.** Twelve states signed the first Geneva Convention,
+providing that the wounded should be collected and cared for regardless of which
+side they belonged to, and that those doing it should be protected. It was signed
+while the Danish war was still being settled, and it was signed because of a
+battle — Solferino, 1859 — that had looked exactly like the ground below Dybbøl
+Mill on 18 April: several thousand wounded men lying in the open with nobody
+whose job it was to fetch them. Denmark acceded the following year.
+
+**Virginia, the same summer.** The last full year of the American Civil War ran
+in parallel with this one, and its scale makes the Danish figures look small: more
+men fell at Gettysburg in three days than Denmark put into the Dybbøl position
+altogether. The connection is not scale but destination. Over the following four
+decades a large number of the young men of the districts this chapter is about
+left for that country, and chapter 35 is partly the story of where they went.
+
+---
+
+## Checkpoints
+
+**Before §04 — where we are.** A king three days on the throne has signed a
+constitution he knows breaks the treaty that made him king. Prussia and Austria
+have crossed the Eider, nobody has come to help, and the army has abandoned the
+Dannevirke without a battle and been vilified for it. The war is five weeks old
+and the position at Dybbøl is not finished.
+
+**Before §07 — where we are.** Dybbøl has fallen, the London conference has broken
+up over a line on a map, Als has been taken in four hours, and Jutland is
+occupied. Denmark has no army left in the field and is about to find out what the
+victors want.
+
+**Before §09 — where we are.** The duchies are ceded, not to Germany but to two
+monarchs personally, and about a fifth of the people who went with them thought of
+themselves as Danes. The Kongeå is a state frontier. Denmark now has two
+constitutions and only one country to apply them to.
+
+---
+
+## Myth-check
+
+**"De Meza abandoned the Dannevirke and lost the war."**
+
+He abandoned it, and the abandonment is the reason there was still a Danish army
+in April. The reputation never recovered, and the record is unambiguous.
+
+The position was indefensible in February 1864 with the force available. It was
+thirty kilometres long, it needed roughly twice the men it had, the Slien had
+frozen so it could be crossed on foot, and the fighting at Mysunde on 2 February
+had already shown that it could be turned. Wrangel's orders from Bismarck and von
+Roon were to destroy the Danish army in front of that line, before it could reach
+Dybbøl and Fredericia. A battle at the Dannevirke was the enemy's plan, not
+Denmark's.
+
+And de Meza was following his instructions. The War Ministry's own paper of 22
+January had told him that the object was to have a capable and battle-ready army in
+the spring, and his council of war on 4 February minuted that reasoning explicitly.
+At one in the morning on 6 February the war minister telegraphed that he did not
+acknowledge that the commanding general had complied with the instruction. He had
+complied with it. He was dismissed within days, took the responsibility publicly,
+and died in 1865.
+
+What actually went wrong was not military. The government had allowed the country
+to believe that the Dannevirke would be held, because the Dannevirke was the
+argument for the war; and when the army did the sensible thing, the government had
+no way to explain it that did not also expose the policy. Somebody had to be
+blamed who was not the cabinet. The crowds who wanted the high command sacked also
+believed the king had ordered the retreat, which he had not.
+
+**A smaller myth, worth killing in passing.** That Denmark lost because the
+Prussians had breech-loading rifles and the Danes did not. The needle gun was real
+and it mattered at Lundby, where it did nothing but kill. It does not explain
+Dybbøl, which was lost to artillery — rifled breech-loading guns emplaced on
+Broager Land on 15 March, outranging every Danish piece on the position, firing
+eight thousand shells in the six hours before a man went forward. The redoubts were
+destroyed before they were stormed. A better rifle would not have rebuilt them.
+
+---
+
+## Carry-forward
+
+**← 19.** Ribe 1460, and the question of what the duchies were to each other and to
+the Danish crown. It is answered here, and not by any of the arguments: the king
+signs his rights away to two foreign monarchs and they divide the spoils.
+
+**← 21.** The partition of 1544, which produced the collateral lines. Both the
+king who signs in 1863 and the pretender who declares against him in Gotha
+descend from it.
+
+**← 33.** The Treaty of London of 1852, the designated heir, and the promise made
+in unpublished notes. §01 is that promise being broken in public by the man the
+treaty put on the throne, and §02 is the bill arriving.
+
+**→ 35.** Two hundred thousand people, or a hundred and seventy thousand, on the
+other side of a new border; the heath; and the emigration.
+
+**→ 36.** The Landsting the 1866 revision created, the deadlock nobody wrote a
+procedure for, and thirty years of constitutional struggle.
+
+**→ Part I.** Article 5 of the Peace of Prague, abrogated in 1878, and the
+plebiscite that finally happens.
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+The Rigsråd passed a constitution for the kingdom and Slesvig on 13 November 1863;
+Frederik 7. died two days later without signing it, at Glücksburg; and Christian 9.,
+king for three days and king only because of the Treaty of London, signed the
+document that broke it.
+
+Nobody came. Britain would not act without France, France wanted paying, Russia had
+no interest in defending a state that had broken the settlement Russia imposed, and
+the German Confederation was acting lawfully until Prussia and Austria stepped
+outside it and crossed the Eider on 1 February 1864.
+
+De Meza abandoned the Dannevirke on the night of 5–6 February, exactly as the
+ministry's own instruction of 22 January implied he should, and was destroyed for
+it; the retreat saved the army. Dybbøl was lost on 18 April to artillery that
+Danish guns could not reach, after more than eight thousand shells in six hours,
+and the casualty figures have never been reconciled.
+
+Denmark then negotiated in London as though it had not lost, refused arbitration,
+and got nothing; Als fell in four hours on 29 June; and at Vienna on 30 October the
+king renounced all three duchies to the Emperor of Austria and the King of Prussia
+personally, who quarrelled over them and fought each other in 1866.
+
+The price at home was paid in 1866, when the revised constitution rebuilt the
+Landsting on a privileged franchise, froze the town-and-country seat distribution
+into the law, and wrote down no procedure for deadlock. That was done by Danes,
+and it produced thirty years of constitutional struggle.
+
+---
+
+## Questions
+
+**Recall.**
+
+1. Why was Christian 9. in a worse position than any other Danish king to sign the
+   November Constitution — and why did he sign it anyway?
+2. What did the War Ministry's instruction of 22 January 1864 tell de Meza, and
+   what did the war minister telegraph him on 6 February?
+3. To whom, precisely, did Denmark cede the duchies at Vienna, and what did the
+   Augustenborg claimant receive?
+
+**Causal.**
+
+1. Denmark expected the great powers to intervene as they had in 1848–50, and none
+   did. What had changed, and when?
+2. Dybbøl is remembered as a battle. In what sense was it decided before the
+   assault began?
+3. The 1866 revision left the Folketing untouched and rebuilt the Landsting. Why
+   was that a more effective change than restricting the Folketing would have been?
+
+**Counterfactual.**
+
+1. Suppose Denmark had accepted arbitration, or a line north of Flensborg, at the
+   London conference in June 1864. What would have been different in 1920?
+2. Suppose Frederik 7. had lived another year and signed the November Constitution
+   himself. Does anything about 1864 change?
+
+**Contested.**
+
+1. Niels Kjeldsen was shot from behind while riding away, and had refused to
+   surrender. One version of that is a hero and the other is not, and Denmark spent
+   thirty years arguing about which. What was actually at stake in the argument,
+   given that both sides agreed on the facts?
+2. This chapter argues that Denmark was not betrayed in 1864 but had put itself in
+   the wrong. What would count as evidence against that reading?
+
+---
+
